questions.explanation:
For federal income taxes, a corporation's charitable contribution deduction is limited to the lesser of the contributions made or 10% of its adjusted taxable income (ATI). ATI is the corporation's taxable income calculated before deducting:

charitable contributions,

any NOL and capital loss carrybacks, and

the dividends received deduction (DRD).

Any disallowed contributions are carried forward for up to five tax years.

In this case, Robin's charitable contribution deduction is $36,250, which is the lesser of the $50,000 charitable contribution made and 10% of its ATI, calculated as:

Revenues | $417,500
+ Dividend income | 20,000
− Operational expenses | (75,000)
ATI | $362,500
× Percent limit | × 10%
Contribution deduction limit | $36,250

